# apex_sdboot — let the boot blessing rename a loader entry on the ESP.
#
# ── the defect this exists for ────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# On the composefs + systemd-boot path the ESP is mounted at /boot, so the
# Type #1 loader entries systemd-bless-boot has to rename are FAT files
# labelled dosfs_t. Measured on the APEX image in the VM lab, SELinux
# enforcing (ROADMAP/evidence/sdboot-image-20260920-decision.md):
#
#   systemd-bless-boot[1609]: Failed to rename
#       '/loader/entries/bootc_fedora-43-1+2-1.conf'
#    to '/loader/entries/bootc_fedora-43-1.conf': Permission denied
#   avc: denied { rename } comm="systemd-bless-b" dev="vda2"
#       scontext=system_u:system_r:init_t:s0
#       tcontext=system_u:object_r:dosfs_t:s0 tclass=file permissive=0
#
# In the same boot, `mv` on the same file in the same directory worked, and
# re-running the same binary from a shell worked. It is the SELinux domain and
# nothing else.
#
# /usr/lib/systemd/systemd-bless-boot is labelled init_exec_t, so PID 1
# executing it leaves the process in init_t. Fedora 43's policy has
# systemd_bless_boot_generator_t for the GENERATOR and no domain at all for the
# worker, and init_t is allowed no rename/create/unlink/open on dosfs_t:file
# and no add_name/write on dosfs_t:dir. It has none of them on boot_t either,
# so mounting the ESP with a context= option would not have helped.
#
# Without this the blessing fails on every boot, the +N-M counter is never
# stripped, and every deployment rolls itself back on its fourth boot. That is
# the exact outcome the boot-path contract exists to prevent.
#
# ── why bootupd_t rather than widening init_t ─────────────────────────────
#
# bootupd_t is the domain Fedora built for a root process that writes
# bootloader state onto the ESP: it already holds full management of dosfs_t
# files and directories, and read access to efivarfs_t, which is what
# systemd-bless-boot needs to read LoaderBootCountPath. Sending the blessing
# there costs one permission. Granting init_t rename on dosfs_t would instead
# hand every service that never leaves init_t write access to every FAT
# filesystem on the machine, including a user's USB stick.
#
# Checked against the live policy with setools rather than assumed:
#
#   init_t -> bootupd_t:process  { transition siginh rlimitinh noatsecure } ALLOW
#   bootupd_t -> init_t:fd use                                             ALLOW
#   bootupd_t -> init_t:unix_stream_socket connectto                       ALLOW
#   bootupd_t -> dosfs_t:file { rename create unlink open read write … }   ALLOW
#   bootupd_t -> init_exec_t:file entrypoint                               DENY  <- the gap
#
# So the module is one rule wide. The transition is only ever requested by the
# SELinuxContext= drop-in this repository ships beside it
# (files/system/units/10-apex-bless-boot-esp.conf); nothing else in the system
# asks to enter bootupd_t through an init_exec_t binary.
#
# THIS MODULE AND THAT DROP-IN ARE A PAIR, AND THE FAILURE IS NOT SYMMETRIC.
# The drop-in's `-` prefix makes a FAILURE TO SET the context non-fatal, which
# covers SELinux being disabled or a policy without bootupd_t. It does NOT
# cover this module going missing on an enforcing machine: setexeccon then
# succeeds and the kernel denies the execve, the unit fails, and the machine
# rolls itself back on the fourth boot. Containerfile.base asserts
# `semodule -l | grep -qx apex_sdboot` for exactly that case.
#
# The long-term fix belongs upstream: systemd-bless-boot should have a domain
# of its own in selinux-policy. When it gets one, delete this module and the
# drop-in together.

# ── why ONLY systemd-bless-boot needs this, and apex-boot-count does not ──
#
# apex-boot-count renames a .conf inside /boot/loader/entries.staged — the same
# FAT ESP, the same dosfs_t, from a systemd ExecStop — so it looks like it
# needs the same treatment. It does not, and the reason is one type_transition:
#
#   type_transition init_t bin_t:process unconfined_service_t;   EXISTS
#   type_transition init_t init_exec_t:process …                 NONE
#
# /usr/libexec/apex-boot-count is bin_t, so systemd runs it in
# unconfined_service_t, which carries files_unconfined_type and renames a
# dosfs_t file unaided. /usr/lib/systemd/systemd-bless-boot is init_exec_t,
# gets no transition, and stays in init_t — which is the whole defect.
#
# This was tried the other way first: a bin_t entrypoint plus a
# SELinuxContext= drop-in on apex-boot-count. That CONFINES a process that was
# already unconfined, into a domain built for a Rust daemon — `bootupd_t ->
# proc_t:file` is `map` only, so the helper could not read /proc/cmdline and
# its own safe-fail would have reported "refusing" forever. Reverted, and
# recorded here so it is not re-added by the next person who notices the
# symmetry.

module apex_sdboot 1.0.0;

require {
    type bootupd_t;
    type init_exec_t;
    class file { entrypoint execute getattr map open read };
}

allow bootupd_t init_exec_t:file { entrypoint execute getattr map open read };
